# AGENTS.md

Rules for any AI client or person working in this repository: Codex, Claude Code, a claude.ai project, or someone using github.com. Read this file first and follow it.

This repository holds articles for one publication, from first draft to published post. Drafts are written here, `ci-review` (from the content-intelligence project) reviews them, and the review comes back into the same folder. Every round is a commit, so the history shows who did what and when.

## Layout

```
AGENTS.md              these rules (managed)
CLAUDE.md              one line, "@AGENTS.md", for Claude Code (managed)
README.md              the same layout and review loop, for people (managed)
.gitattributes         "* text=auto eol=lf" (managed)
templates/             the file shapes: draft_submission.template.md, metadata_only.md,
                       publication.md, close_out.md, revise_after_review_prompt.md (managed)
instructions/
  claude-project.md    the stanza pasted at the top of the claude.ai project's instructions (managed)
  publication.md       the publication's own drafting rules: voice and editorial (the author's)
.ci-docs/manifest.json what the bootstrap wrote, and from which source commit
articles/<slug>/       one folder per article
  <slug>-draft-rN.md
  <slug>-metadata-rN.md
  <slug>-handoff-rN.md
  <slug>-publication-rN.md
  <slug>-close-out-rN-to-code.md, <slug>-close-out-rN-to-chat.md
  <slug>-review-rN.md
  pipeline/            the worklist and the run reports
```

"Managed" means the file is written by `ci-docs init` from the content-intelligence repository. Do not edit a managed file here: the edit is lost or refused the next time the bootstrap runs. Change its source in content-intelligence and re-run `ci-docs init`. Everything else in this repository is yours to edit as these rules allow. `instructions/publication.md` is the exception to "managed": it was seeded once and belongs to the author.

## Names

`<slug>` is the article's folder name and the stem of every file in it. It is lowercase words joined by hyphens, and it never changes.

| File | Name | Written by |
|---|---|---|
| draft | `<slug>-draft-rN.md` | the drafting side |
| metadata | `<slug>-metadata-rN.md` | the drafting side |
| draft and metadata in one file | `<slug>-handoff-rN.md` | the drafting side |
| publication handoff | `<slug>-publication-rN.md` | the drafting side |
| review | `<slug>-review-rN.md` | `ci-review` |

A close-out note is named `<slug>-close-out-rN-to-(code|chat)[-vM].md` and either side may write it: `to-code` is addressed to a Code session, `to-chat` to the drafting chat.

`N` is a round number, counted from 1. On a draft, a metadata file, a handoff and a publication handoff it is the round of drafting. On a review and on a close-out note `N` is the pipeline run number: `<slug>-review-r4.md` is run 4's review. `-vM` on a close-out note is a rewrite of the same round and direction, `-v2`, `-v3`, and the note says which one it supersedes.

### Close-out notes

A close-out note is written from `templates/close_out.md` and saved in the article's folder. Before writing one:

1. **List the folder first** (the GitHub MCP `get_file_contents` on `articles/<slug>/`, or `ci-docs ls <slug>`). If a note for this round and direction is already there, you are writing its `-vM` successor, and its `Supersedes:` line names the note it replaces.
2. **Name it** `<slug>-close-out-rN-to-(code|chat)[-vM].md`. `N` is the last pipeline run the note takes into account, the `N` of `<slug>-review-rN.md`. The first note for a round and direction has no `-v`; the next is `-v2`, then `-v3`. A `-vM` needs `-v(M-1)` to be there already (`-v2` needs the plain note).
3. **Never overwrite one.** A note that is there stays there, whatever is wrong with it.

A Code session adds a note with `ci-docs put <slug> <file>`, which applies these same rules: it refuses a name that does not fit the pattern, a `-vM` with no predecessor, and a round and direction that already has a note written without `-v` (it suggests the next free one). A person using github.com or a claude.ai connector has to apply them by hand.

## Where to commit

- Commit to `main` only. Do not create branches, and never force-push.
- One commit per step, with these messages:

| Commit | Message |
|---|---|
| a draft and its metadata, together in one commit | `draft: <slug> rN` |
| a handoff | `handoff: <slug> rN` |
| a close-out note | `close-out: <slug> rN to-code` or `close-out: <slug> rN to-chat`, with ` v2`, ` v3` added for a rewrite |
| a publication handoff | `publication: <slug> rN` |
| a review | `review: <slug> rN`, which is the pipeline's |

## Submitting a round for review

1. Write `<slug>-draft-rN.md` and `<slug>-metadata-rN.md` (or one `<slug>-handoff-rN.md`) from the shapes in `templates/`.
2. Commit them together in one commit: `draft: <slug> rN`. With GitHub's MCP connector that is the `push_files` tool, which writes several files in one commit; `create_or_update_file` makes one commit per file, so using it for the pair leaves a commit that holds a draft with no metadata. On github.com, upload both files in one commit.
3. Ask the author to run `ci-review --github <slug>`. You cannot run the review yourself.

`History key:` is optional here. When a handoff or metadata file has none, the run uses the article's folder name, `<slug>`, as its history key: the line in the templates that says the title becomes the key does not apply to a round read from this repository. If you do write a `History key:`, make it the slug, because the pipeline lowercases a key and cuts it to 60 characters, and a key that differs from the folder name splits the article's history in two.

## Where the review comes back

The review is `<slug>-review-rN.md` at the top level of the article's folder, in a commit the pipeline makes. In the claude.ai project it appears after "Sync now". Revise the draft with `templates/revise_after_review_prompt.md`: paste the review under that prompt, and write the result as the next round's files, `rN+1`.

The pipeline also writes `pipeline/<slug>-worklist-rN.md` and the run's `report.json` under `pipeline/`. Those are the author's. Do not read them into a revision.

## Reading a pipeline commit

A commit whose message starts `review: <slug> rN` was made by the pipeline. Its `Reviewed:` line names the draft files it reviewed and the commit they were at, so you can tell which version of the draft a review is about even after the draft has changed. If the draft at that commit is not the draft in front of you, the review may not apply to it.

## Never

- Rename or move an article folder.
- Modify or delete a numbered file. A new round is a new file. A close-out note that needs rewriting gets a `-vM` successor; the old one stays.
- Rewrite history or force-push.
- Commit a secret, a token or a credential. This repository is private, and a secret committed once stays in its history.
- Read `pipeline/` (the worklist and the reports) into a revision.
- Edit a managed file here. Its source is in content-intelligence.

## Publication-specific rules

Voice, editorial standards and anything else particular to this publication are in `instructions/publication.md`, when it exists. This file covers the workflow; that one covers the writing.
